<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Bulimia nervosa, severe, in a 28-year-old woman who asked for help after about three years of binge eating with self-induced vomiting, now about ten times a week, with low potassium and moderate depression. Please confirm the diagnosis and severity, advise on the level of care, the psychological treatment, and fluoxetine, and set the medical monitoring and the thresholds for a higher level of care. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within one week, on the same day as her one-week primary care visit. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Do you agree with the diagnosis of bulimia nervosa, severe, and with co-occurring recurrent major depression?</item><item>Is outpatient care safe, and what findings should prompt urgent medical care or a higher level of eating-disorder care?</item><item>Which psychological treatment do you advise: guided self-help or eating-disorder-focused CBT?</item><item>Should fluoxetine start now or only if psychotherapy alone has not helped by six weeks, and at what dose?</item><item>What medical monitoring do you advise for her electrolytes, ECG, and teeth while she is still vomiting?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Spencer, a 28-year-old charity worker, is seen at the request of Dr. Castleford one week after she first asked for help. For about three years she has restricted her eating through the day and binged in the evenings, each binge followed by self-induced vomiting; this week there were seven episodes, from about ten the week before. The binges bring brief comfort and then shame. She judges herself largely by her shape and weight. She has never used laxatives, diuretics or diet pills and does not exercise to compensate. She has had no fainting, palpitations, weakness, or blood in the vomit. For about two months she has been low, tired, and hopeless, and she had an earlier depression after childbirth. She has no current thoughts of suicide or self-harm; past self-injury is part of her history, and there has been none for years. She drinks a little at weekends and uses no drugs. She wants to stop and is willing to start treatment now. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>She was born The Honourable Diana Frances Spencer on 1 July 1961 at Park House, near Sandringham in Norfolk, England. [D]</item><item>On 29 July 1981 she married the Prince of Wales at St Paul&apos;s Cathedral in London. [D]</item><item>During her marriage she was president or patron of more than 100 charities, and she drew attention to homelessness, disability, children&apos;s causes, and HIV/AIDS. [D]</item></list><paragraph>7 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

