<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Bulimia nervosa, severe (F50.23). She meets the DSM-5-TR criteria: recurrent binge eating with a sense of loss of control, recurrent self-induced vomiting to prevent weight gain, both at least weekly for more than three months, and self-evaluation unduly shaped by weight and shape, without the low weight of anorexia nervosa. With about seven to ten compensatory episodes a week it is severe. Outpatient care is safe: potassium has risen to 3.6 mmol/L on oral replacement, the QTc is below 460 ms, she has not fainted, and the pulse rise on standing is modest. Because the illness is severe and has lasted years, eating-disorder-focused CBT (CBT-E) is a better choice than guided self-help. Fluoxetine 60 mg daily has the most evidence in bulimia nervosa and also treats her depression, so it should start now rather than after six weeks of psychotherapy alone. Bupropion should be avoided because of the risk of seizures in bulimia nervosa. [S]</item><item>Major depressive disorder, recurrent, moderate (F33.1). A moderate depressive episode, recurrent given the earlier depression after childbirth. It often improves as bingeing and vomiting settle; fluoxetine treats both. Her risk of self-harm is higher with both conditions, so the safety plan should be reviewed at every visit, especially in the first weeks of fluoxetine. [S]</item><item>Hypokalemia from vomiting (E87.6). Mild and improving on oral replacement. It should be checked weekly until normal twice and then at each visit while she vomits; magnesium should be checked again, because low magnesium keeps potassium low. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Castleford&apos;s intake note, safety assessment, and safety plan. Intake: potassium 3.1, chloride 94, bicarbonate 32 mmol/L; magnesium 1.8 and phosphate 3.4 mg/dL; amylase 146 U/L; creatinine 0.84 mg/dL; QTc 452 ms; PHQ-9 14; SCOFF 4 of 5; EDE-Q global score 3.9. Today: potassium 3.6, chloride 99, bicarbonate 28 mmol/L, creatinine 0.80 mg/dL; PHQ-9 13; weight 61.2 kg, BMI 19.3; her self-monitoring record for the week. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Outpatient care. Arrange urgent medical care, or a higher level of eating-disorder care, for potassium below 3.0 mmol/L despite replacement, QTc above 500 ms or new arrhythmia, fainting, a resting pulse below 50, vomiting she cannot control, blood in the vomit, or suicidal thoughts. [S]</item><item>Eating-disorder-focused CBT (CBT-E): 20 sessions over 20 weeks with an eating disorders therapist, twice weekly for the first four weeks, starting this week, with regular eating, weighing in session, and a relapse-prevention plan at the end. [S]</item><item>Start fluoxetine 20 mg each morning, increase to 40 mg after one week and 60 mg after two weeks; a nurse phone check one to two weeks after starting for side effects, agitation, and suicidal thoughts; continue for at least a year after remission. Avoid bupropion. [S]</item><item>Continue potassium chloride 20 mEq twice daily until potassium is normal on two checks; electrolytes and creatinine weekly until then and at each medical visit while vomiting continues; repeat magnesium at the next check. [S]</item><item>Repeat the ECG two weeks after reaching fluoxetine 60 mg, because potassium has been low. [S]</item><item>A dental examination soon, with no brushing for an hour after vomiting, a non-acid fluoride mouthwash, and fewer acidic drinks. [S]</item><item>Continue the eating disorders dietitian for regular eating; no dieting. [S]</item><item>Measure the PHQ-9 at each visit and the EDE-Q at mid-treatment and at the end; review safety at every visit. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Castleford, who sees her the same day. The plan was explained to Ms. Spencer, who agreed to start CBT-E and fluoxetine; she decides who else receives this note. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Psychiatry review at the end of CBT-E if she is not in remission, and review of a fluoxetine taper after a year of remission. Dr. Castleford continues prescribing and medical monitoring. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
